{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '600,667p' packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts && rg -n -C 5 '12\\\\.|coincident' reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.fix.report.md && rg -n '7\\\\. \\\\*\\\\*|coincident|I1 scatter' reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core3.interaction.review.md reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/acceptance-browser.report.md packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/i1-pointer.browser.test.ts\"","aggregated_output":"  /** x as the reader sees it. */\n  readonly x: string;\n  /** The value with its unit, or \"No data\" / \"No observation\". */\n  readonly value: string;\n  readonly state: SlotState;\n  /** Share of the whole for a donut slice, formatted; null elsewhere. */\n  readonly percent: string | null;\n  /** Source row index, or -1. */\n  readonly row: number;\n}\n\nexport interface TooltipModel {\n  /** Heading: the x value (with its time zone for instants), the category, or the slice label. */\n  readonly title: string;\n  /** At most eight rows (spec I1). */\n  readonly rows: readonly DatumText[];\n  /** Rows before truncation. */\n  readonly count: number;\n  /** Stack total with \"incomplete\" where it applies, already formatted; null when the chart does not stack. */\n  readonly total: string | null;\n}\n\nexport type StepDirection = 'next' | 'previous' | 'first' | 'last' | 'series-next' | 'series-previous' | 'page-next' | 'page-previous';\n\n/**\n * Exact answers from the frame for one view and projection, independent of how the scene was simplified.\n * Built once per (view, layout); cheap to build: binary search over x for aligned frames, a grid index for scatter.\n */\nexport interface Lookup {\n  /** What is under a pixel of the plot container, or null. */\n  pick(px: number, py: number): PickTarget | null;\n  tooltip(target: PickTarget): TooltipModel;\n  /** The datum to focus when the keyboard takes over from a pointer target. */\n  primary(target: PickTarget): DatumRef | null;\n  datum(ref: DatumRef): DatumText | null;\n  /** Where the datum is drawn, for the cursor and focus ring; null if it is not visible. */\n  anchor(ref: DatumRef): Rect | null;\n  /**\n   * Position for \"point 4 of 120\" (spec A3). Aligned charts: `count` is the number of visible x slots and\n   * `ordinal` the zero-based place of the datum's x among them, whatever its series and whether or not it\n   * holds a value (a \"No data\" slot has a place too). Scatter: place among all visible points in x then y\n   * order. Donut: slice order. -1 only when the datum is not in the current view.\n   */\n  readonly count: number;\n  ordinal(ref: DatumRef): number;\n  /** Keyboard order per chart type (spec 3.6). `from` null starts at the first datum. Skips hidden series. */\n  step(from: DatumRef | null, direction: StepDirection): DatumRef | null;\n  /**\n   * Where focus should be after the data or the view changed. A datum is \"there\" only if its series is\n   * visible and drawable and its x (or point) is inside the current view. If it is: itself. If its series is\n   * hidden but its x is in view: the primary datum at that x. Otherwise the nearest datum of its series in\n   * view after it, else before it; for a donut slice folded into \"Other\", the \"Other\" slice. Else null.\n   * `step` always starts from the resolved datum, so focus can never get stuck on something out of reach.\n   */\n  resolve(ref: DatumRef): DatumRef | null;\n}\n\n// ───────────────────────────── reader model ─────────────────────────────\n\n/** The table of spec A4, built lazily from the frame: nothing is materialized until a page is asked for. */\nexport interface TableModel {\n  readonly caption: string;\n  /** First entry is the x column. */\n  readonly headers: readonly string[];\n  readonly rowCount: number;\n  readonly pageSize: number;\n  /** Cells of one row as text; first cell is the row header. null outside the range. */\n  row(ordinal: number): readonly string[] | null;\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.fix.report.md-11-| 7. Empty ranges | Yes | Yes | One no-data sentence per series, no unavailable value range, and an empty zoom. |\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.fix.report.md-12-| 8. Donut summary | Yes | Yes | Drawn-slice count, total, formatted shares, and grouped categories named only through Other. |\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.fix.report.md-13-| 9. Undrawable series | Yes | Yes | Duplicate-x series appears in the summary even when absent from the visibility mask. |\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.fix.report.md-14-| 10. References | Yes | Yes | Lines and bands use their axis formatters and appear in both summary and caption. |\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.fix.report.md-15-| 11. Scatter steps | Yes | Yes | Recreated refs with shuffled source rows and a binary-search window for switching series. |\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.fix.report.md:16:| 12. Scatter scaling | Yes | No, partial | Successor search, distant table paging, and clustered picking are covered by counters. The worst-case pointer candidate cap remains open. |\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.fix.report.md-17-| 13. Parsing and formatting | Yes | Yes | Supplied percentage formatter plus calendar, instant, and wall-clock jumps. The data index lacks a standalone x-position parser, so the adapter uses its exported one-row normalization path. |\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.fix.report.md-18-| 14. End steps and folded slices | Yes | Yes | Steps that cannot move return `null`. Folded refs resolve through Other’s members. |\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.fix.report.md-19-| 15. Regression coverage | Yes | Yes | Widened properties use partial ranges, hidden series, and hand-built duplicate-x scatter frames. Reversed irregular-time picking already passed before the fix. |\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.fix.report.md-20-\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.fix.report.md-21-Scatter ref lookup costs `O(log n + k)` for an equal-x run of length `k`. Next and previous use one binary search per visible series. A 100-row page across 500 series took one seek and 100 heap advances. Pointer picks visit at most 16 grid cells once, and the tested dense fixtures stayed below 3,000 point-distance checks. A separate ring fixture straddling the 0.5 px coincidence boundary took 200,033 checks. Exact coincidence counting can still visit every leaf that intersects that boundary.\n--\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-51-- **Ran:** `labelledBy: 'h'` gives `table.caption === ''`.\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-52-- **Spec:** A4 says \"the title as caption\". An empty caption leaves the table unnamed.\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-53-- **Fix:** take the caption as its own input (the generated name, or the heading text the caller supplies). Never `''`.\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-54-\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-55-**4. M: the F12 note says changed values are \"not shown\"**\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md:56:- **Where:** reader/index.ts:121; messages/index.ts:11-12.\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-57-- **Ran:** `disclosure {omitted: 0, changed: 1}` gives \"1 values not shown. See table.\" reader/index.test.ts:51 pins this string.\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-58-- **Inferred:**\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-59-  - AX121 is reported with effect `'changed'` (view/donut.ts). A donut with 12 categories would therefore read \"5 values not shown\" while those values are drawn inside \"Other (5)\".\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-60-  - `valuesChanged` exists in Messages but nothing in src uses it.\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-61-  - Counts go through `String()`, so \"1234\", not the locale's \"1,234\" (F6).\n--\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-117-  - scatter.ts:20-22: any ref that did not come from this lookup sorts all rows once. That covers every ref after a resize, zoom or append rebuild. It costs 17-20 ms for 200,000 points with unsorted rows, on top of an 8 ms grid build.\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-118-- **Fix:**\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-119-  - Locate a ref with `ref.x`: binary search on `series.x`, then scan the run of equal x for the row.\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-120-  - `series-next`: binary search the target series by x and look at a pixel window (or use the grid).\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-121-\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md:122:**12. m: cost grows faster than linearly with series count, and with clustered points**\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-123-- **Ran, `pointOrder.at` (point-order.ts:23-54) on 200,000 points:**\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-124-\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-125-  | Series | ms per call |\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-126-  |---|---|\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-127-  | 8 | 0.05 |\n--\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-130-  | 2,000 | 81 |\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-131-\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-132-  Every `next` and every table `row(i)` calls it. A 100-row table page therefore takes about 0.7 s with 500 series and about 8 s with 2,000.\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-133-- **Ran, scatter-grid.ts:42-85:**\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-134-  - 200,000 points in one cell: each pointer move examines 400,000 candidates (2.4 ms).\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md:135:  - All points coincident: 4.3 ms.\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-136-  - The coincidence pass walks the same cells again.\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-137-- **Fix:** `next`/`previous` as a successor search (O(m log n)); table pages through a k-way merge cursor; merge the two grid passes and split dense cells.\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-138-\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-139-**13. m: cross-module coupling and a silent locale fallback**\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-140-- **Where and what:**\n--\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-163----\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-164-\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-165-**Areas I tried hard to break and could not**\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-166-- **Aligned picking:** exact midpoints go to the later slot on forward and reversed ranges, including irregular time spacing. Also checked: both plot edges, a single slot, zoomed range, horizontal bars by y, a pointer outside the plot (ran).\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-167-- **Bar anchors:** they match the scene's rectangles exactly for grouped bars, stacked bars with mixed signs, stacked horizontal bars and date-axis bars (ran).\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md:168:- **Scatter grid:** 20,000 random in-plot queries against brute force gave zero mismatches. These covered points on 24 px cell boundaries, an offset plot, non-square scales and negative coordinates. Ties went to the later row, and coincident points were capped at 8 with the full count of 12 (ran).\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-169-- **`pointOrder`:** `at` and `ordinal` against brute force over 2,000 fast-check runs, with partial ranges and duplicates: no failure (ran).\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-170-- **Donut:** angle picks at slice boundaries and on both sides of 12 o'clock, the hole and ring edges, the Other slice, arcless slices in keyboard order, and percentages were all correct (ran).\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-171-- **Table:** a 1,000,000-row model builds in 0.4 ms and `row(999999)` is O(1). `pageForX` is right at page boundaries, before and after the range, and for date, time and category inputs (ran).\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-172-- **`fill` and `resolveMessages`:** braces, `$&` and the placeholder syntax inside values are not re-interpreted; `{toString}` is left alone; single-key overrides work (ran).\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/lookup-reader.review.md-173-- **Aligned eviction:** `resolve` picks the next surviving datum, then the previous, and returns null for a vanished series (ran).\npackages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/i1-pointer.browser.test.ts:129:      it(`I1 ${type}: with ten series ${type === 'scatter' ? 'on one coincident point' : 'at one x'} the tooltip lists eight rows and \"+2 more\"; in a 260 px tile the same content appears in the fixed readout under the plot`, async () => {\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/acceptance-browser.report.md:73:5. **I1 scatter: one point cannot be picked.**\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core3.interaction.review.md:17:| 7 | **M · Partly fixed. Ran.** | A hidden-only x is reachable, ordinary stacked totals are present, and Right stays in the chosen scatter series. With ten coincident points, the pointer returned eight refs and count ten. Focusing the earliest row failed the membership check and produced one ref and count one. With ten stacked series and one gap, the spoken text contained `incomplete` **three times**. Preserve the full coincidence count even when the focused ref falls outside the eight displayed rows. Format overflow series without adding the stack qualification to each one. **I1, A2, A3, §3.6.** [interaction.ts:316](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:316) [focus.ts:137](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/focus.ts:137) |\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core3.interaction.review.md:48:7. **M · Keyboard zoom-in can zoom out. Ran.** On a line with x values `0,10,…,100`, set a supported public window `[40,45]`. The gesture minimum is 20. At center 42.5, the zoom-in calculation returned `[32.5,52.5]`. The toolbar disables Zoom in, but `+` and inward wheel still call `scaled`, which accepts any different result. Require a strictly narrower result for zoom-in and a strictly wider result for zoom-out. Apply the same decision to wheel consumption. **I3, I4.** [focus.ts:285](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/focus.ts:285) [interaction.ts:629](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:629)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
